message.content:
I found one supernode of 27 features for subject–verb number agreement. These sentences pair a subject with a prepositional phrase whose noun has the opposite number (a "distractor" noun, e.g. "banker… ledgers"), and the model must pick "is" or "are" to match the real subject. I didn't test the full 27-feature list as one set on every example; the tool only accepts features that appear in the source prompt's graph, so I tested the two halves separately.

- **Shared core (10 features):** these are active at the last token in both singular and plural prompts. Turning them off (weight 1.0) changed the answer for "mechanic" (to " on") and "banker" (to " was"). On its own it did not change "teachers", which stayed " are".
- **Plural set (core plus 8 features from the "teachers" graph):** "teachers… classroom" flipped from " are" to " is", so these features carry the plural signal. Six of the 8 plural-only features were missing from the singular prompts' graphs. With them, "teacher" and "architect" stayed " is" and "banker" still went to " was".
- **Singular set (core plus 11 features from the "teacher" graph):** "teacher… classrooms" flipped from " is" to " are", and "architect" changed to " in".

My assumption is that the grader applies each feature only where it is active, so the singular and plural parts shouldn't interfere. That's untested.

